WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.380
A kidney cell does not make synapses.

2
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:10.320
Can one neuronal protein persuade an axon to build a terminal on it?

3
00:00:11.233 --> 00:00:13.333
Neurexin sits on the axon.

4
00:00:14.153 --> 00:00:16.233
Neuroligin is its partner on the dendrite.

5
00:00:17.367 --> 00:00:23.187
In 2000, Peter Scheiffele and Tito Serafini's laboratory made kidney cells produce

6
00:00:23.187 --> 00:00:24.187
neuroligin.

7
00:00:24.900 --> 00:00:27.540
They grew these cells under axons from the pons.

8
00:00:28.583 --> 00:00:33.763
Where an axon crossed a neuroligin cell, synaptic vesicle proteins gathered into

9
00:00:33.763 --> 00:00:34.763
clusters.

10
00:00:35.233 --> 00:00:40.393
When the axons were depolarized, the clusters took up antibodies to the inside face

11
00:00:40.393 --> 00:00:41.533
of a vesicle protein.

12
00:00:42.493 --> 00:00:44.593
So vesicles were being released and retrieved.

13
00:00:45.617 --> 00:00:47.737
Control cells did not do this.

14
00:00:48.537 --> 00:00:52.257
Soluble beta-neurexin, which binds neuroligin, blocked the effect.

15
00:00:53.267 --> 00:00:55.547
The signal also runs the other way.

16
00:00:56.347 --> 00:01:00.227
Neurexin on a bare cell clusters postsynaptic proteins in dendrites.

17
00:01:04.650 --> 00:01:09.610
So neuroligin is sufficient, under these conditions, to start a terminal on a bare

18
00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:10.610
cell.

19
00:01:10.883 --> 00:01:11.983
But is it necessary?

20
00:01:13.167 --> 00:01:18.667
Predict. Mice lack the alpha-neurexins, or neuroligins 1, 2 and 3.

21
00:01:19.547 --> 00:01:23.187
Fewer synapses, normal ones, or synapses that are there but fail?

22
00:01:29.650 --> 00:01:30.950
You need two measurements.

23
00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:33.990
Count the synapses, and record what they do.

24
00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:40.790
In 2003, Markus Missler, Thomas Südhof and colleagues reported mice lacking all

25
00:01:40.790 --> 00:01:42.050
three alpha-neurexins.

26
00:01:43.083 --> 00:01:46.183
Excitatory synapses were near normal in density.

27
00:01:47.143 --> 00:01:49.403
Inhibitory synapses were moderately fewer.

28
00:01:50.467 --> 00:01:52.747
And the synapses released transmitter poorly.

29
00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:02.000
In 2006, mice lacking neuroligins 1, 2 and 3 were reported.

30
00:02:02.980 --> 00:02:05.100
Brainstem synapses were normal in number.

31
00:02:06.150 --> 00:02:09.950
But transmission was impaired, more so at inhibitory synapses.

32
00:02:14.483 --> 00:02:16.583
Add stargazer, from the first film.

33
00:02:17.563 --> 00:02:20.263
Counting found most synapses in place in every mouse.

34
00:02:21.223 --> 00:02:23.123
Recording found something wrong in every one.

35
00:02:24.233 --> 00:02:27.293
This does not mean deletions never change number.

36
00:02:28.253 --> 00:02:32.293
Deleting every neurexin from some interneurons does lower synapse number.

37
00:02:33.367 --> 00:02:35.267
Why would the brain manage without it?

38
00:02:36.167 --> 00:02:38.087
One likely reason is overlap.

39
00:02:39.067 --> 00:02:43.307
Several organizers can start contacts, so removing one family leaves others.

40
00:02:44.417 --> 00:02:49.517
Another is that some organizers specify or check contacts, rather than start them.

41
00:02:50.467 --> 00:02:53.407
Takeaway: a dish tests what one protein can start.

42
00:02:54.307 --> 00:02:56.367
A mouse tests what the brain cannot do without.

43
00:02:57.307 --> 00:02:58.947
Measure function, as well as count.
